 "wiki": {
  "title": "Abronia lythrochila",
  "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abronia_lythrochila",
  "revision": 1376668507,
  "sections": [
   {
    "h": null,
    "level": 1,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila, also known commonly as the red-lipped arboreal alligator lizard and escorpión arboricola  de labios rojos in Mexican Spanish, is a species of lizard in the family Anguidae. The species is native to southern Mexico."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Geographic distribution",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila is endemic to the state of Chiapas in Mexico."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Habitat",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila lives in montane dry pine-oak forest, at an elevation of 2,000–3,000 m (6,600–9,800 ft)."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Conservation status",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila has a relatively small geographic range but is common within it. The species is threatened by deforestation, but much of its range is in protected areas, including Lagunas de Montebello National Park."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Description",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila can reach a total length (tail included) of about 7–11 in (18–28 cm). The basic body color is greenish-olive, with irregular dark blotches. There are spiny horn-like scales on each side of the head. The lower labials are blood-red, to which the common names refer. The tail is prehensile."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Diet",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila preys predominately upon insects."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Reproduction",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Abronia lythrochila is ovoviviparous."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
